#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175803 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/id #uuid "8c4777a6-d831-54cc-8677-b05ea45b2b4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7324" :question/problem "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with a particular virus  follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} =  4P - 0.000\\,01  P^2,  \\qquad P(0) = 5\\,000,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus outbreak began. For which value of $P$ is the number of people infected with the virus growing at the fastest possible rate?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\nP' = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P = \\dfrac12a.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =4P-0.000\\,01{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,01P(400\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 400\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $400\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac12a = \\dfrac12\\cdot 400\\,000 = 200\\,000.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175875 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/id #uuid "6f96059e-ce3d-5771-b3d8-15044cfd5495" :question/math-academy-id "e-9375" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of some foxes in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\ny' =0.5y - 0.002y^2, \\qquad y(0) = 25,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible fox population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.5y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(250-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 250.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $250,$ and we conclude that the number of foxes cannot exceed $250.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175876 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176021 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/id #uuid "2096869c-2a41-51a5-b2e8-c1039af4d640" :question/math-academy-id "e-9376" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y = y(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny' = 2y\\left(60 - \\frac{y}{3} \\right), \\qquad y(0) = 10\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)}?$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y(60-\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\  & =2y(\\frac{180-y}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{3}(180-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 180.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $180.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)} = 180.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "342e6f1b-ac3e-50b2-98d7-df8f0229b2e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480195} {:answer/id #uuid "50f68122-9efd-5a7b-939d-e58e9cf110e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "650" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480249} {:answer/id #uuid "65462c67-cb94-5a4f-aef1-c3b35dc9c272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480273} {:answer/id #uuid "9c43af4b-b5aa-5fb8-9285-24a4affe03dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480368} {:answer/id #uuid "fcdf8785-d333-580f-b2a8-20410c69ffa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcdf8785-d333-580f-b2a8-20410c69ffa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480523} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c5e5ed9-14e9-5160-bdc0-df76ba3efee1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c2c81b1-ac73-5714-9490-1d6081f1f637" :question/math-academy-id "q-118554" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of deer in a certain forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.6y-0.0012{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=120,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of deer?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.6y-0.001\\,2{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0012y(500-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=500.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $500,$ and we conclude that the number of deer cannot exceed $500.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d9155d4-a4cc-59e5-bd9f-c69415302b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174099} {:answer/id #uuid "89df3b37-9b87-5ad8-94c0-1562f3045626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480342} {:answer/id #uuid "e2cc4215-cc25-5cee-9728-a8b87958cd6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480486} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a0621d-a491-583d-9c70-88361bf8cc17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480510} {:answer/id #uuid "f70fd909-f6a9-551d-a0f6-60ff3aaa4ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89df3b37-9b87-5ad8-94c0-1562f3045626" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480342} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d04916e-2a37-50d5-b3db-87246180c681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82fbd2ba-8dc6-52ea-8a84-7a3c5cc31fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-118557" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of turtles on a certain island follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1.5y-0.001{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=500,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of turtles?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1.5y-0.001{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.001y(1\\,500-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1\\,500.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1\\,500,$ and we conclude that the number of turtles cannot exceed $1\\,500.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176021 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50c0b70e-50e7-5f65-8762-82b8b0a4a949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480247} {:answer/id #uuid "8197e250-af8d-5840-a654-e65f3c5a61d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480327} {:answer/id #uuid "82224bdd-18cd-5e5b-a183-bd1ca7b2348b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480328} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d5cd07-0714-5ffe-ad5d-03e1ea2cdec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480447} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c430d7-093b-52e9-83ca-91b039a4280d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82224bdd-18cd-5e5b-a183-bd1ca7b2348b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480328} :answer-field/id #uuid "0117cb33-8b7c-57f9-952f-780b347d7a96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "99513b0e-90cb-593a-b1cd-0a535510b802" :question/math-academy-id "q-118575" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=900y-6{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=35\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=6y(150-y),\\,y(0)=35.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=150.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $150.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176021 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bcd10b0-e620-5625-b4e2-6780c884442e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480180} {:answer/id #uuid "495c9db5-3511-570d-a54b-70f28b1735b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480235} {:answer/id #uuid "5e12866c-8fd9-5d70-9397-0511278a760d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480269} {:answer/id #uuid "6b797d09-5401-5a5c-b2b6-fed4fc476b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480285} {:answer/id #uuid "7fddda94-c503-5b3c-830c-5ac4964c1dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "495c9db5-3511-570d-a54b-70f28b1735b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480235} :answer-field/id #uuid "c51adab7-d9bc-58f1-9bfb-406f118e6c90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2304365e-e658-571a-a752-03df743e6d8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-118578" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,500y-12{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=15\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=12y(125-y),\\,y(0)=15.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=125.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $125.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176021 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e648c9a-926a-5f59-9853-589fa9d9d8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250809} {:answer/id #uuid "736ac0bf-0dbe-5e68-9494-dc20cab9dc48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251504} {:answer/id #uuid "99f6f0f3-641e-5e3c-9f53-a5dd99a5934a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251876} {:answer/id #uuid "c21bdf6c-9a6b-59cc-a86d-935920b73c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252266} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d8d39e-d9ee-5f7d-b8f8-1a4ec2e6d553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c21bdf6c-9a6b-59cc-a86d-935920b73c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252266} :answer-field/id #uuid "52cd9670-efaa-5997-a26c-de39051a6431" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edf79f32-6c49-50fc-bc9d-59df10630ea5" :question/math-academy-id "q-118581" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=720y-8{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=5\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8y(90-y),\\,y(0)=5.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=90.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $90.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176021 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081d2611-c9d5-5ff8-acf0-23903af25f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480126} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb6f314-795c-5293-badb-a09dd380b034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480182} {:answer/id #uuid "4004a3d0-2cbf-51cf-a3c3-743093b7b616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480215} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac27f65-90ba-5e1a-903c-2209418975d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480313} {:answer/id #uuid "a7813003-46c8-549a-951a-80403b679f4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "081d2611-c9d5-5ff8-acf0-23903af25f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480126} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9c885db-a51b-565f-802d-b5e3e3429fb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da1f1140-0b6f-5e8e-98c1-c5bc7c260a47" :question/math-academy-id "q-118587" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y=y(t):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9y(1-\\frac{y}{1\\,200}),\\,y(0)=200\n$$\n\nWhat is ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =9y(1-\\frac{y}{1\\,200}) \\\\  & =9y(\\frac{1\\,200-y}{1\\,200}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9y}{1\\,200}(1\\,200-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{400}(1\\,200-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1\\,200.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1\\,200.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)=1\\,200.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176021 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd0eb52-cc0f-562f-be51-1c6de89f1f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480136} {:answer/id #uuid "763eb809-8ab8-5de0-b78d-c70965684ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480303} {:answer/id #uuid "a2cccbb1-9a1e-5817-a3e7-962d276dd0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480382} {:answer/id #uuid "a83e929b-9573-595a-83ff-0453f1512e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480394} {:answer/id #uuid "d181188d-9045-57fe-a84f-1e62b139b7fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dd0eb52-cc0f-562f-be51-1c6de89f1f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480136} :answer-field/id #uuid "a97f6f8c-e126-5e61-838a-7e75c12192eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "548860d7-a3cd-5a87-9ba4-81882cafa76b" :question/math-academy-id "q-119840" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,800y-2{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=30\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y(900-y),\\,y(0)=30.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=900.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $900.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40813bed-c283-5998-b908-85358de0d46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251091} {:answer/id #uuid "a4190740-8ee6-56ce-b602-223c593c07ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251968} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b0a46a-f606-5c39-a1c6-f00d88e31929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251981} {:answer/id #uuid "c93f40e7-f250-55a6-84c1-de5f6d5ba56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252332} {:answer/id #uuid "e94476cf-4519-5af3-b99e-898e3687a889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40813bed-c283-5998-b908-85358de0d46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251091} :answer-field/id #uuid "da13ec6c-83c7-50f9-876b-4ab3d2ef21e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "819cfa7f-e175-52e2-b6a4-00c7d6cd6241" :question/math-academy-id "q-119849" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of giraffes on a certain African wildlife reserve follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=17.2y-0.004{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=180,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of giraffes?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =17.2y-0.004{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.004y(4\\,300-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=4\\,300.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $4\\,300,$ and we conclude that the number of giraffes cannot exceed $4\\,300.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0594f223-200c-542c-be81-07e72759ac03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480120} {:answer/id #uuid "0b70aa2b-729a-5da7-a35a-50ae0199754c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480132} {:answer/id #uuid "4341d8a3-0f2b-5ec4-84db-379db13471bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480222} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb22c01-113f-5dc7-bea5-a610d7aec30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480449} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c01786-caa1-5695-9de3-f30704bb60b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0594f223-200c-542c-be81-07e72759ac03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480120} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ab0258f-7c09-5528-925a-c8ae2bfa6440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e06f869-86f4-5afe-a28b-daef542c7fe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-15486" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of squirrels in a certain forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,5P(6\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=1\\,000\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the squirrel population growing at the fastest possible rate?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFrom our equation, we see that $a=6\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $6\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\,000=3\\,000.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b823059-32a5-5517-a7b7-fb48a969e9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b823059-32a5-5517-a7b7-fb48a969e9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480241} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c112df9-705c-527b-8556-06919619c93f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480568}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "990eaa9e-cd8b-5349-b27e-24596fc286e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-353469" :question/problem "The number $y(t)$ of homes in a city that have adopted clean energy follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y(20-\\frac{y}{125}),\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy?\n\nThe maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible number of clean-energy-adopting homes in the city. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8133f1c4203903bac606bbae61c73717e2ba2dc15a97a77f48eaaeaf4736d06.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3y(20-\\frac{y}{125}) \\\\  & =3y(\\frac{2\\,500-y}{125}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{125}(2\\,500-y) \\\\  & =0.024y(2\\,500-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2500.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2500,$ and we conclude that the maximum possible number of homes that will adopt clean energy is $2500.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "733dd2ee-a0f5-5037-97ab-545d842b762d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "733dd2ee-a0f5-5037-97ab-545d842b762d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480299} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d4c9911-0775-5294-9352-eb815ab61b7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21fb3dfd-c1e0-5974-85b5-71a08b2e3884" :question/math-academy-id "q-353476" :question/problem "The number $y(t)$ of people in a closed community who have been infected with a virus follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2400y-3{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=12,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible number of infected people?\n\nThe maximum possible number of infected people is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible number of people who will be infected. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8133f1c4203903bac606bbae61c73717e2ba2dc15a97a77f48eaaeaf4736d06.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2400y-3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3y(800-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=800.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $800,$ and we conclude that the maximum possible number of infected people is $800.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8351bd80-e518-5ece-9f96-abee1eb4c668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8351bd80-e518-5ece-9f96-abee1eb4c668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319789} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b990bb7-090c-58a0-b6cc-f6b6c5614b8c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "542b4bd1-a5e6-5564-a6fe-12f10ea4329d" :question/math-academy-id "q-353483" :question/problem "The download count $P(t)$ of a viral mobile game follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=4P(8\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=600.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the download count growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/90/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFrom our equation, we see that $a=8000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $8000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8000=4000.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480671 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dc6f863-6eeb-5db4-ad8c-e70735286a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dc6f863-6eeb-5db4-ad8c-e70735286a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480184} :answer-field/id #uuid "429ec1f3-edbf-511d-9966-3b57d80b0385" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "780f42b2-e05c-5d59-ba5e-7cf6b84ac19f" :question/math-academy-id "q-353491" :question/problem "The number $P(t)$ of employees at a growing company follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=9600P-8{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=150,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the number of employees growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/90/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =9600P-8{P}^{2} \\\\  & =8P(1200-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1200.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1200,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1200=600.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101ea620-7c24-59db-9258-511ec049dc5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330089} {:answer/id #uuid "255d3436-78af-5f95-8551-acbf5c7be1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330271} {:answer/id #uuid "605d8033-5655-5ed0-906f-5023ddbf89d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330804} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cc0c21-5861-5a8b-a662-8d40db92437b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331587} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0152f8-d567-5785-9344-68fb05ce830a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca0152f8-d567-5785-9344-68fb05ce830a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331739} :answer-field/id #uuid "1706aa51-cd5b-516d-bad0-ce97d9d20daa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7dc96cac-3ced-5c6f-a9e4-3db7edb477b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-51379" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$of rabbits in a particular forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=4P-0.002{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the rabbit population growing at the fastest possible rate?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =4P-0.002{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002P(2\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\,000=1\\,000.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191a8fdb-4cf1-52b9-b39d-877ed2bfcf4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480150} {:answer/id #uuid "1fe55633-1a01-5334-8af1-1c9155c4685b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480164} {:answer/id #uuid "226ece79-c20c-52a6-9ef4-69ce428c1b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480168} {:answer/id #uuid "7075748d-27ae-566a-8e0a-bfe33ec489f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480292} {:answer/id #uuid "786cef4c-88dd-5537-bc9a-2d9fbba7feed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "191a8fdb-4cf1-52b9-b39d-877ed2bfcf4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480150} :answer-field/id #uuid "963b2bae-6d0b-579e-ad6a-b3bc97461b9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a97fa6ce-6558-5992-afd2-fa81f89839cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-85982" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of turtles  on a certain island follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=4.8P-0.001\\,2{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=200,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the turtle population growing at the fastest possible rate?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =4.8P-0.001\\,2{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.001\\,2P(4\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=4\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $4\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\,000=2\\,000.\n$$"}]}
